basename() is declared before it is called

gdial-plat-util.c calls basename() with no declaration of it in sight:
<string.h> declares it only under _GNU_SOURCE. The C compiler then takes its
result to be an int. On 32-bit ARM that is the whole pointer. On a 64-bit
machine the pointer loses its upper half, and the first message the library
logs ends the process with a segmentation fault in fprintf(): the XCast
plug-in's process, a few seconds after Thunder starts.

Upstream-Status: Pending [not yet offered to rdkcentral/xdialserver]

--- a/server/plat/gdial-plat-util.c	2026-11-23 22:40:30
+++ b/server/plat/gdial-plat-util.c	2026-11-23 22:40:30
@@ -17,6 +17,11 @@
  * limitations under the License.
  */
 
+/* For basename() of <string.h>: without a declaration its result is taken
+ * to be an int, and a pointer of 64 bits loses its upper half. */
+#ifndef _GNU_SOURCE
+#define _GNU_SOURCE
+#endif
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
